{"name":"Silver Star","hex":"#98a0b8","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lustrous, white, metallic element, atomic number 47, atomic weight 107.87, symbol Ag.","Coins made from silver or any similar white metal."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from silver.","Made from another white metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver"},{"term":"star","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any small, natural and bright dot in the sky, most visible in the night or twilight sky. This sense includes the planets, but it is now sometimes used in exclusion of them due to influence from the technical astronomical sense.","A planet thought to influence one's fate; (figuratively) fate or luck."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To appear as a featured performer or headliner, especially in an entertainment program.","To feature (a performer or a headliner), especially in a movie or an entertainment program."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/star"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver Star","blocks":[{"text":"The Silver Star Medal (SSM) is the United States Armed Forces' third-highest military decoration for valor in combat. The Silver Star Medal is awarded primarily to members of the United States Armed Forces for gallantry in action against an enemy of the United States."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/Silver_Star_medal.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Star"},{"title":"Silver Star (Amtrak train)","blocks":[{"text":"The Silver Star is a temporarily suspended long-distance passenger train operated by Amtrak on a 1,522-mile (2,449 km) route between New York City and Miami via Washington, D.C.; Richmond, Virginia; Raleigh, North Carolina; Columbia, South Carolina; Savannah, Georgia; Jacksonville, Florida; and Tampa, Florida."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Amtrak_Silver_Star_92_in_SOP.jpg/330px-Amtrak_Silver_Star_92_in_SOP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Star_(Amtrak_train)"},{"title":"Star","blocks":[{"text":"A star is a luminous spheroid of plasma held together by self-gravity. The nearest star to Earth is the Sun. Many other stars are visible to the naked eye at night; their immense distances from Earth make them appear as fixed points of light. The most prominent stars have been categorised into constellations and asterisms, and many of the brightest stars have proper names. Astronomers have assembled star catalogues that identify the known stars and provide standardized stellar designations. The observable universe contains an estimated 1022 to 1024 stars. Only about 4,000 of these stars are visible to the naked eye—all within the Milky Way galaxy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg/330px-The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Silver_Star_Medal_Reverse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Silver_Star_Medal_Reverse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilver_Star_Medal_Reverse.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Mullen_Awards_Silver_Star_to_Capt._Ambrosia.JPG/500px-Mullen_Awards_Silver_Star_to_Capt._Ambrosia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Mullen_Awards_Silver_Star_to_Capt._Ambrosia.JPG/1280px-Mullen_Awards_Silver_Star_to_Capt._Ambrosia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Mullen_Awards_Silver_Star_to_Capt._Ambrosia.JPG/500px-Mullen_Awards_Silver_Star_to_Capt._Ambrosia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Army Captain Gregory Ambrosia receiving the Silver Star from Navy Admiral Michael Mullen, Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMullen_Awards_Silver_Star_to_Capt._Ambrosia.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Cheney_Presents_Silver_Star_to_Spc._Monica_Brown.jpg/500px-Cheney_Presents_Silver_Star_to_Spc._Monica_Brown.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Cheney_Presents_Silver_Star_to_Spc._Monica_Brown.jpg/1280px-Cheney_Presents_Silver_Star_to_Spc._Monica_Brown.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Cheney_Presents_Silver_Star_to_Spc._Monica_Brown.jpg/500px-Cheney_Presents_Silver_Star_to_Spc._Monica_Br